\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Average curvature on a made-up artery with unevenly spaced points, computed with the thesis code. The curvature is the inverse radius $1/R$ of a circle (A), and at each point $P_i$ it is computed with Menger's formula from the triangle of $P_i$ and the points $B_i$ and $F_i$ 5\,mm away (B), so a straight stretch gives 0 and a tighter bend gives a smaller circle and a larger curvature (C). The values form the curvature profile, which has no value near the ends of the artery, and the average curvature $T$ of each segment is the area under its profile divided by the length it covers (D).\relax }}{30}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:method-curvature}{{6.1}{30}{Average curvature on a made-up artery with unevenly spaced points, computed with the thesis code. The curvature is the inverse radius $1/R$ of a circle (A), and at each point $P_i$ it is computed with Menger's formula from the triangle of $P_i$ and the points $B_i$ and $F_i$ 5\,mm away (B), so a straight stretch gives 0 and a tighter bend gives a smaller circle and a larger curvature (C). The values form the curvature profile, which has no value near the ends of the artery, and the average curvature $T$ of each segment is the area under its profile divided by the length it covers (D).\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
\newlabel{fig:method-curvature@cref}{{[figure][1][6]6.1}{[1][29][]30}}
